\chapter{Reading MuDSL.hpp and MuDSL.d together}
The source appendices include the actual C++ header and D module. They expose
the same toolkit ideas through each host's native composition mechanisms.
Understanding their differences prevents mechanically translating an API into
an ownership or evaluation bug.

\section{Composition and staging}
C++ uses feature tags and CRTP mixins to build a concrete DSL type. Concepts and
requires-expressions reject unsupported operations during compilation. Fixed
strings permit identifiers and grammar fragments to become template arguments.
D uses template mixins, alias parameters, static foreach, and compile-time
function evaluation. Strings are already legal template arguments, so a separate
fixed-string wrapper is optional. In both hosts, concrete callable types allow
inlining and retain captured state without mandatory type erasure.

\section{Parsers and trees}
Parser combinators retain success/failure and remaining-input information.
Repetition must make progress; a parser accepting an empty string cannot be
blindly repeated. PEG choice is ordered and greedy, whereas the CFG Earley
parser represents explicit grammar productions and supports ambiguity. Choose
the parser whose commitment rules match the language rather than attempting to
repair ambiguities after evaluation.

AST nodes, first-order terms, term graphs, and e-graphs have different contracts.
ASTs preserve syntactic order. Term unification binds variables structurally.
Term graphs share nodes. E-graphs represent equivalence classes and congruence;
they do not encode sequencing effects by themselves. MuLLIER therefore retains
ordered children and ships only effect-preserving transformations.

\section{Serialization, I/O, and visitors}
Serializer/deserializer adapters borrow user-supplied backends and dispatch to
their protocols. The byte I/O adapter supports short reads and writes; clients
must distinguish end-of-file from incomplete transfer. Visitors can be derived
from runtime trees or schemas. These are independent facilities: adopting the
MuLL interpreter does not replace or disable the toolkit's parser, graph,
reflection, regular-expression, preprocessing, and task-pipeline APIs.

\section{Host-specific lifetime rules}
C++ native functions are shared callable objects, while environments belong to
a runtime-owned arena. A function value must not outlive its runtime. D uses
delegates and garbage-collected environments, but retaining a delegate also
retains its captures. Neither implementation makes a single runtime thread-safe.
Host callbacks can call a MuLL function argument; reentering \api{run} is rejected.

C++ includes \api{mull/mullier/Passes.hpp} from the public toolkit header. D
imports \api{mull.mullier.MuLLIER}, whose toolkit imports are explicit. Link the
D MuLLIER modules along with \api{MuDSL.d}; templates alone do not supply the
runtime pass implementations. The installed CMake interface includes all required
C++ headers, and installed D sources retain their module-relative directories.
